    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p53-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the derivative function of $y=\\dfrac{x-\\alpha}{x-\\beta}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{\\alpha-\\beta}{(x-\\beta)^2}$, $x\\ne\\beta$.",
          "worked": "$u=x-\\alpha$, $u'=1$; $v=x-\\beta$, $v'=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac{u'v-uv'}{v^2}\\\\&=\\frac{1(x-\\beta)-(x-\\alpha)1}{(x-\\beta)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{x-\\beta-x+\\alpha}{(x-\\beta)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{\\alpha-\\beta}{(x-\\beta)^2},\\qquad x\\ne\\beta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 33
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that for $\\alpha>\\beta$, all tangents have a positive gradient, and for $\\alpha<\\beta$, all tangents have a negative gradient.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $x\\ne\\beta$, $(x-\\beta)^2>0$, so $y'$ has the same sign as $\\alpha-\\beta$. Thus $\\alpha>\\beta$ gives positive tangent gradients, and $\\alpha<\\beta$ gives negative tangent gradients.",
          "worked": "For every $x$ in the domain, $(x-\\beta)^2>0$. Therefore the sign of $y'=\\dfrac{\\alpha-\\beta}{(x-\\beta)^2}$ is determined by its numerator.\nIf $\\alpha>\\beta$, then $\\alpha-\\beta>0$, so $y'>0$ and every tangent has a positive gradient.\nIf $\\alpha<\\beta$, then $\\alpha-\\beta<0$, so $y'<0$ and every tangent has a negative gradient.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p53-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What happens when $\\alpha=\\beta$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=1$ and $y'=0$ for $x\\ne\\beta$, so all tangents are horizontal. The function is undefined at $x=\\beta$.",
          "worked": "When $\\alpha=\\beta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{x-\\beta}{x-\\beta}\\\\&=1,\\qquad x\\ne\\beta,\\\\y'&=\\frac{0}{(x-\\beta)^2}\\\\&=0,\\qquad x\\ne\\beta.\\end{align*}$$\nThe graph is the horizontal line $y=1$ with a hole at $(\\beta,1)$. Every tangent is horizontal; there is no tangent at the excluded point.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
  },
